    "number": 20,
    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Forces",
    "prompt": "A telephone pole has three cables pulling as shown from above, with F → 1 = ( 300.0 i ^ + 500.0 j ^ ) , F → 2 = −200.0 i ^ , and F → 3 = −800.0 j ^ . (a) Find the net force on the…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A telephone pole has three cables pulling as shown from above, with <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>300.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>500.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>−200.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>−800.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. (a) Find the net force on the telephone pole in component form. (b) Find the magnitude and direction of this net force.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A telephone pole has three cables pulling as shown from above, with <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>300.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>500.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>−200.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>−800.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. (a) Find the net force on the telephone pole in component form. (b) Find the magnitude and direction of this net force.</p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"Figure shows the coordinate axes, vector F1 at an angle of about 28 degrees with the positive y axis, vector F2 along the negative x axis and vector F3 along the negative y axis.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/e292371739e6cf8d0b3ef46c.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-problems#fs-id1165038995758",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 5, Problem 20. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-problems#fs-id1165038995758\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
