    "level": 2,
    "imported": true,
    "source": "OpenStax",
    "volume": 1,
    "chapter": 9,
    "number": 115,
    "category": "challenge-problems",
    "topic": "Momentum and collisions",
    "prompt": "Two projectiles of mass m 1 and m 2 are ﬁred at the same speed but in opposite directions from two launch sites separated by a distance D . They both reach the same spot in their highest…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Two projectiles of mass <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> are ﬁred at the same speed but in opposite directions from two launch sites separated by a distance <em>D</em>. They both reach the same spot in their highest point and strike there. As a result of the impact they stick together and move as a single body afterwards. Find the place they will land.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Two projectiles of mass <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> are ﬁred at the same speed but in opposite directions from two launch sites separated by a distance <em>D</em>. They both reach the same spot in their highest point and strike there. As a result of the impact they stick together and move as a single body afterwards. Find the place they will land.</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-challenge-problems#fs-id1167132415455",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 9, Problem 115. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-challenge-problems#fs-id1167132415455\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
