    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Moment of Inertia and Rotational Kinetic Energy",
    "prompt": "A baseball pitcher throws the ball in a motion where there is rotation of the forearm about the elbow joint as well as other movements. If the linear velocity of the ball relative to the…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A baseball pitcher throws the ball in a motion where there is rotation of the forearm about the elbow joint as well as other movements. If the linear velocity of the ball relative to the elbow joint is 20.0 m/s at a distance of 0.480 m from the joint and the moment of inertia of the forearm is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0.500</mn><msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>kg-m</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, what is the rotational kinetic energy of the forearm?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A baseball pitcher throws the ball in a motion where there is rotation of the forearm about the elbow joint as well as other movements. If the linear velocity of the ball relative to the elbow joint is 20.0 m/s at a distance of 0.480 m from the joint and the moment of inertia of the forearm is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0.500</mn><msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>kg-m</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, what is the rotational kinetic energy of the forearm?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/10-problems#fs-id1167133328918",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-10#fs-id1167133328918-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>\n<span><math display=\"inline\">\n<semantics>\n<mrow>\n<mrow>\n<mi>K</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mn>434.0</mn>\n<mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace>\n<mtext>J</mtext>\n</mrow>\n</mrow>\n\n</semantics>\n</math></span>\n</p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 10, Problem 57. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/10-problems#fs-id1167133328918\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
