    "number": 43,
    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Conductors, Insulators, and Charging by Induction",
    "prompt": "Suppose a speck of dust in an electrostatic precipitator has 1.0000 × 10 12 protons in it and has a net charge of −5.00 nC (a very large charge for a small speck). How many electrons…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Suppose a speck of dust in an electrostatic precipitator has <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1.0000</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> protons in it and has a net charge of −5.00 nC (a very large charge for a small speck). How many electrons does it have?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Suppose a speck of dust in an electrostatic precipitator has <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1.0000</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> protons in it and has a net charge of −5.00 nC (a very large charge for a small speck). How many electrons does it have?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-problems#fs-id1172102001985",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/chapter-5#fs-id1172102001985-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p><span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.00</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−9</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6.242</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>18</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mtext>e</mtext><mtext>/</mtext><mtext>C</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3.121</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>;<span><br/></span>\n<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3.121</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>e</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>+</mtext><mn>1.0000</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>e</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0312</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p></div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 5, Problem 43. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-problems#fs-id1172102001985\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
