    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Model of Conduction in Metals",
    "prompt": "An aluminum wire 1.628 mm in diameter (14-gauge) carries a current of 3.00 amps. (a) What is the absolute value of the charge density in the wire? (b) What is the drift velocity of the…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>An aluminum wire 1.628 mm in diameter (14-gauge) carries a current of 3.00 amps. (a) What is the absolute value of the charge density in the wire? (b) What is the drift velocity of the electrons? (c) What would be the drift velocity if the same gauge copper were used instead of aluminum? The density of copper is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>8.96</mn><msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>g/cm</mtext></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and the density of aluminum is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2.70</mn><msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>g/cm</mtext></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. The molar mass of aluminum is 26.98 g/mol and the molar mass of copper is 63.5 g/mol. Assume each atom of metal contributes one free electron.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>An aluminum wire 1.628 mm in diameter (14-gauge) carries a current of 3.00 amps. (a) What is the absolute value of the charge density in the wire? (b) What is the drift velocity of the electrons? (c) What would be the drift velocity if the same gauge copper were used instead of aluminum? The density of copper is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>8.96</mn><msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>g/cm</mtext></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and the density of aluminum is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2.70</mn><msup><mrow><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>g/cm</mtext></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. The molar mass of aluminum is 26.98 g/mol and the molar mass of copper is 63.5 g/mol. Assume each atom of metal contributes one free electron.</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-problems#fs-id1170902924394",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 9, Problem 28. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-problems#fs-id1170902924394\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
