    "topic": "Newton’s laws",
    "prompt": "A body of mass m moves in a horizontal direction such that at time t its position is given by x ( t ) = a t 4 + b t 3 + c t , where a , b , and c are constants. (a) What is the…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A body of mass <em>m</em> moves in a horizontal direction such that at time <em>t</em> its position is given by <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> where <em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, and <em>c</em> are constants. (a) What is the acceleration of the body? (b) What is the time-dependent force acting on the body?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A body of mass <em>m</em> moves in a horizontal direction such that at time <em>t</em> its position is given by <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> where <em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, and <em>c</em> are constants. (a) What is the acceleration of the body? (b) What is the time-dependent force acting on the body?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-challenge-problems#fs-id1165039433374",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 5, Problem 96. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-challenge-problems#fs-id1165039433374\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
