    "category": "challenge-problems",
    "topic": "Capacitance",
    "prompt": "A spherical capacitor is formed from two concentric spherical conducting shells separated by a vacuum. The inner sphere has radius 12.5 cm and the outer sphere has radius 14.8 cm. A…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A spherical capacitor is formed from two concentric spherical conducting shells separated by a vacuum. The inner sphere has radius 12.5 cm and the outer sphere has radius 14.8 cm. A potential difference of 120 V is applied to the capacitor. (a) What is the energy density at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12.6</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, just outside the inner sphere? (b) What is the energy density at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14.7</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, just inside the outer sphere? (c) For the parallel-plate capacitor the energy density is uniform in the region between the plates, except near the edges of the plates. Is this also true for the spherical capacitor?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A spherical capacitor is formed from two concentric spherical conducting shells separated by a vacuum. The inner sphere has radius 12.5 cm and the outer sphere has radius 14.8 cm. A potential difference of 120 V is applied to the capacitor. (a) What is the energy density at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12.6</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, just outside the inner sphere? (b) What is the energy density at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14.7</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, just inside the outer sphere? (c) For the parallel-plate capacitor the energy density is uniform in the region between the plates, except near the edges of the plates. Is this also true for the spherical capacitor?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-challenge-problems#fs-id1167131394473",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 8, Problem 80. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-challenge-problems#fs-id1167131394473\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
