    "prompt": "The magnetic flux through the loop shown in the accompanying figure varies with time according to Φ m = 2.00 e −3 t sin ( 120 π t ) , where Φ m is in webers. What are the direction and…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>The magnetic flux through the loop shown in the accompanying figure varies with time according to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Φ</mtext><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.00</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>−3</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mtext>sin</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>120</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> where <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Φ</mtext><mtext>m</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is in webers. What are the direction and magnitude of the current through the <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.00</mn><mtext>-</mtext><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> resistor at (a) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>; (b) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.17</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mtext>−</mtext><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and (c) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.00</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s?</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>The magnetic flux through the loop shown in the accompanying figure varies with time according to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Φ</mtext><mtext>m</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.00</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>−3</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mtext>sin</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>120</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> where <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>Φ</mtext><mtext>m</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is in webers. What are the direction and magnitude of the current through the <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.00</mn><mtext>-</mtext><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> resistor at (a) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>; (b) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.17</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mtext>−</mtext><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and (c) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.00</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s?</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p><span>\n<img alt=\"Figure shows a loop with the magnetic flux perpendicular to the loop. Loop is connected to a 5 Ohm resistor.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/3ad1881fbeb836ae90c1a53a.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-problems#fs-id1171242190325",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/chapter-13#fs-id1171242190325-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>a. 150 A downward through the resistor; b. 46 A upward through the resistor; c. 0.019 A downward through the resistor</p></div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 13, Problem 35. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/13-problems#fs-id1171242190325\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
