    "topic": "Work and energy",
    "prompt": "Consider a linear spring, as in Figure 7.7 (a), with mass M uniformly distributed along its length. The left end of the spring is fixed, but the right end, at the equilibrium position x…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Consider a linear spring, as in <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-1-work\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Figure 7.7</a>(a), with mass <em>M</em> uniformly distributed along its length. The left end of the spring is fixed, but the right end, at the equilibrium position <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is moving with speed <em>v</em> in the <em>x</em>-direction. What is the total kinetic energy of the spring? (<em>Hint:</em> First express the kinetic energy of an infinitesimal element of the spring <em>dm</em> in terms of the total mass, equilibrium length, speed of the right-hand end, and position along the spring; then integrate.)</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Consider a linear spring, as in <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-1-work\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Figure 7.7</a>(a), with mass <em>M</em> uniformly distributed along its length. The left end of the spring is fixed, but the right end, at the equilibrium position <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is moving with speed <em>v</em> in the <em>x</em>-direction. What is the total kinetic energy of the spring? (<em>Hint:</em> First express the kinetic energy of an infinitesimal element of the spring <em>dm</em> in terms of the total mass, equilibrium length, speed of the right-hand end, and position along the spring; then integrate.)</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-challenge-problems#fs-id1165036974100",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 7, Problem 108. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-challenge-problems#fs-id1165036974100\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
