    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Model of Conduction in Metals",
    "prompt": "The current supplied to an air conditioner unit is 4.00 amps. The air conditioner is wired using a 10-gauge (diameter 2.588 mm) wire. The charge density is n = 8.48 × 10 28 electrons m 3…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>The current supplied to an air conditioner unit is 4.00 amps. The air conditioner is wired using a 10-gauge (diameter 2.588 mm) wire. The charge density is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8.48</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>28</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><mrow><mtext>electrons</mtext></mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. Find the magnitude of (a) current density and (b) the drift velocity.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>The current supplied to an air conditioner unit is 4.00 amps. The air conditioner is wired using a 10-gauge (diameter 2.588 mm) wire. The charge density is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8.48</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>28</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><mrow><mtext>electrons</mtext></mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. Find the magnitude of (a) current density and (b) the drift velocity.</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-problems#fs-id1170902685224",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/chapter-9#fs-id1170902685224-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>a. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>7.60</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></msup><mfrac><mtext>A</mtext><mrow><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>; b. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>d</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5.60</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−5</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><mtext>m</mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mfrac></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 9, Problem 33. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-problems#fs-id1170902685224\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
