    "topic": "Types of Collisions",
    "prompt": "In an elastic collision, a 400-kg bumper car collides directly from behind with a second, identical bumper car that is traveling in the same direction. The initial speed of the leading…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>In an elastic collision, a 400-kg bumper car collides directly from behind with a second, identical bumper car that is traveling in the same direction. The initial speed of the leading bumper car is 5.60 m/s and that of the trailing car is 6.00 m/s. Assuming that the mass of the drivers is much, much less than that of the bumper cars, what are their final speeds?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>In an elastic collision, a 400-kg bumper car collides directly from behind with a second, identical bumper car that is traveling in the same direction. The initial speed of the leading bumper car is 5.60 m/s and that of the trailing car is 6.00 m/s. Assuming that the mass of the drivers is much, much less than that of the bumper cars, what are their final speeds?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-problems#fs-id1167134591442",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-9#fs-id1167134591442-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>the speed of the leading bumper car is 6.00 m/s and that of the trailing bumper car is 5.60 m/s</p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 9, Problem 47. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-problems#fs-id1167134591442\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
