    "topic": "Work and Power for Rotational Motion",
    "prompt": "A sphere of mass 1.0 kg and radius 0.5 m is attached to the end of a massless rod of length 3.0 m. The rod rotates about an axis that is at the opposite end of the sphere (see below).…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A sphere of mass 1.0 kg and radius 0.5 m is attached to the end of a massless rod of length 3.0 m. The rod rotates about an axis that is at the opposite end of the sphere (see below). The system rotates horizontally about the axis at a constant 400 rev/min. After rotating at this angular speed in a vacuum, air resistance is introduced and provides a force <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0.15</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> on the sphere opposite to the direction of motion. What is the power provided by air resistance to the system 100.0 s after air resistance is introduced?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A sphere of mass 1.0 kg and radius 0.5 m is attached to the end of a massless rod of length 3.0 m. The rod rotates about an axis that is at the opposite end of the sphere (see below). The system rotates horizontally about the axis at a constant 400 rev/min. After rotating at this angular speed in a vacuum, air resistance is introduced and provides a force <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0.15</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> on the sphere opposite to the direction of motion. What is the power provided by air resistance to the system 100.0 s after air resistance is introduced?</p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"Figure shows a sphere attached to the end of a rod. The rod rotates about an axis that is at the opposite end of the sphere.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/ac085a2c7666ae48c43faf34.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/10-problems#fs-id1167134910554",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 10, Problem 102. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/10-problems#fs-id1167134910554\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
