    "prompt": "Small bodies of mass m 1 and m 2 are attached to opposite ends of a thin rigid rod of length L and mass M . The rod is mounted so that it is free to rotate in a horizontal plane around a…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Small bodies of mass <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> are attached to opposite ends of a thin rigid rod of length <em>L</em> and mass <em>M</em>. The rod is mounted so that it is free to rotate in a horizontal plane around a vertical axis (see below). What distance <em>d</em> from <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> should the rotational axis be so that a minimum amount of work is required to set the rod rotating at an angular velocity <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>?</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Small bodies of mass <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> are attached to opposite ends of a thin rigid rod of length <em>L</em> and mass <em>M</em>. The rod is mounted so that it is free to rotate in a horizontal plane around a vertical axis (see below). What distance <em>d</em> from <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> should the rotational axis be so that a minimum amount of work is required to set the rod rotating at an angular velocity <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>?</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"Figure shows a thin rod of length L that has masses m1 and m2 connected to the opposite ends. The rod rotates around the axis that passes through it at a d distance from m1 and L-d distance from m2.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/d5c8693c9781a579150d089a.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/10-problems#fs-id1167131112931",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 10, Problem 106. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/10-problems#fs-id1167131112931\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
