    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Pendulums",
    "prompt": "A pendulum with a period of 2.00000 s in one location ( g = 9.80 m/s 2 ) is moved to a new location where the period is now 1.99796 s. What is the acceleration due to gravity at its new…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A pendulum with a period of 2.00000 s in one location (<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9.80</mn><msup><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>) is moved to a new location where the period is now 1.99796 s. What is the acceleration due to gravity at its new location?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A pendulum with a period of 2.00000 s in one location (<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9.80</mn><msup><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>) is moved to a new location where the period is now 1.99796 s. What is the acceleration due to gravity at its new location?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/15-problems#fs-id1167130202347",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-15#fs-id1167130202347-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>\n<span><math display=\"inline\">\n<semantics>\n<mrow>\n<mrow>\n<mn>9.82002</mn>\n<mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace>\n<msup>\n<mrow>\n<mtext>m/s</mtext>\n</mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</msup>\n</mrow>\n</mrow>\n\n</semantics>\n</math></span>\n</p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 15, Problem 49. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/15-problems#fs-id1167130202347\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
