    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Superconductors",
    "prompt": "Electrical power generators are sometimes “load tested” by passing current through a large vat of water. A similar method can be used to test the heat output of a resistor. A R = 30 Ω…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Electrical power generators are sometimes “load tested” by passing current through a large vat of water. A similar method can be used to test the heat output of a resistor. A <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> resistor is connected to a 9.0-V battery and the resistor leads are waterproofed and the resistor is placed in 1.0 kg of room temperature water <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>°</mtext><mtext>C</mtext></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. Current runs through the resistor for 20 minutes. Assuming all the electrical energy dissipated by the resistor is converted to heat, what is the final temperature of the water?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Electrical power generators are sometimes “load tested” by passing current through a large vat of water. A similar method can be used to test the heat output of a resistor. A <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> resistor is connected to a 9.0-V battery and the resistor leads are waterproofed and the resistor is placed in 1.0 kg of room temperature water <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>°</mtext><mtext>C</mtext></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. Current runs through the resistor for 20 minutes. Assuming all the electrical energy dissipated by the resistor is converted to heat, what is the final temperature of the water?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-problems#fs-id1170903039341",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 9, Problem 66. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-problems#fs-id1170903039341\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
