    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Equipotential Surfaces and Conductors",
    "prompt": "Shown below are two concentric spherical shells of negligible thicknesses and radii R 1 and R 2 . The inner and outer shell carry net charges q 1 and q 2 , respectively, where both q 1…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Shown below are two concentric spherical shells of negligible thicknesses and radii <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> The inner and outer shell carry net charges <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> respectively, where both <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> are positive. What is the electric potential in the regions (a) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> (b) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and (c) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>?</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Shown below are two concentric spherical shells of negligible thicknesses and radii <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> The inner and outer shell carry net charges <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> respectively, where both <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> are positive. What is the electric potential in the regions (a) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> (b) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and (c) <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>?</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"The figure shows two concentric spheres with radii R subscript 1 and R subscript 2.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/aa2578cb8ba41cdd5798cefa.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-problems#fs-id1167586030716",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 7, Problem 66. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-problems#fs-id1167586030716\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
