    "topic": "Mass and Weight",
    "prompt": "When the Moon is directly overhead at sunset, the force by Earth on the Moon, F EM , is essentially at 90 ° to the force by the Sun on the Moon, F SM , as shown below. Given that F EM =…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>When the Moon is directly overhead at sunset, the force by Earth on the Moon, <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mtext>EM</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, is essentially at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>90</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> to the force by the Sun on the Moon, <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mtext>SM</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, as shown below. Given that <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mtext>EM</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.98</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mtext>SM</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4.36</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> all other forces on the Moon are negligible, and the mass of the Moon is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>7.35</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>22</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>kg</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> determine the magnitude of the Moon’s acceleration.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>When the Moon is directly overhead at sunset, the force by Earth on the Moon, <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mtext>EM</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, is essentially at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>90</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> to the force by the Sun on the Moon, <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mtext>SM</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, as shown below. Given that <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mtext>EM</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.98</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mtext>SM</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4.36</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> all other forces on the Moon are negligible, and the mass of the Moon is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>7.35</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>22</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>kg</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> determine the magnitude of the Moon’s acceleration.</p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"Figure shows a circle labeled moon. An arrow from it, pointing up is labeled F subscript EM. Another arrow from it pointing right is labeled F subscript SM.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/96ad618f93aa52f4f5729c89.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-problems#fs-id1165038378293",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 5, Problem 50. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-problems#fs-id1165038378293\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
