    "type": "FRQ",
    "level": 1,
    "imported": true,
    "source": "OpenStax",
    "volume": 1,
    "chapter": 3,
    "number": 52,
    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Motion with Constant Acceleration",
    "prompt": "(a) A light-rail commuter train accelerates at a rate of 1.35 m/s 2 . How long does it take to reach its top speed of 80.0 km/h, starting from rest? (b) The same train ordinarily…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>(a) A light-rail commuter train accelerates at a rate of 1.35 m/s<sup>2</sup>. How long does it take to reach its top speed of 80.0 km/h, starting from rest? (b) The same train ordinarily accelerates opposite to the motion at a rate of 1.65 m/s<sup>2</sup>. How long does it take to come to a stop from its top speed? (c) In emergencies, the train can accelerate opposite to the motion more rapidly, coming to rest from 80.0 km/h in 8.30 s. What is its emergency acceleration in meters per second squared?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>(a) A light-rail commuter train accelerates at a rate of 1.35 m/s<sup>2</sup>. How long does it take to reach its top speed of 80.0 km/h, starting from rest? (b) The same train ordinarily accelerates opposite to the motion at a rate of 1.65 m/s<sup>2</sup>. How long does it take to come to a stop from its top speed? (c) In emergencies, the train can accelerate opposite to the motion more rapidly, coming to rest from 80.0 km/h in 8.30 s. What is its emergency acceleration in meters per second squared?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-problems#fs-id1168329315924",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 3, Problem 52. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-problems#fs-id1168329315924\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
