    "number": 64,
    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Coulomb's Law",
    "prompt": "Point charges q 1 = 10 μ C and q 2 = −30 μ C are fixed at r 1 = ( 3.0 i ^ − 4.0 j ^ ) m and r 2 = ( 9.0 i ^ + 6.0 j ^ ) m . What is the force of q 2 on q 1 ?",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Point charges <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mi>μ</mi><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>−30</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mi>μ</mi><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> are fixed at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>3.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>i</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>j</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>9.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>i</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>6.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>j</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mtext>m</mtext><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> What is the force of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>on</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Point charges <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mi>μ</mi><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>−30</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mi>μ</mi><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> are fixed at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>3.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>i</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>j</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>9.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>i</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>6.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>j</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mtext>m</mtext><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> What is the force of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>on</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-problems#fs-id1172097198688",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 5, Problem 64. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-problems#fs-id1172097198688\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
