    "topic": "Motion with Constant Acceleration",
    "prompt": "Unreasonable results At the end of a race, a runner accelerates opposite to the motion from a velocity of 9.00 m/s at a rate of 2.00 m/s 2 . (a) How far does she travel in the next 5.00…",
    "previewHTML": "<p><strong>Unreasonable results</strong> At the end of a race, a runner accelerates opposite to the motion from a velocity of 9.00 m/s at a rate of 2.00 m/s<sup>2</sup>. (a) How far does she travel in the next 5.00 s? (b) What is her final velocity? (c) Evaluate the result. Does it make sense?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p><strong>Unreasonable results</strong> At the end of a race, a runner accelerates opposite to the motion from a velocity of 9.00 m/s at a rate of 2.00 m/s<sup>2</sup>. (a) How far does she travel in the next 5.00 s? (b) What is her final velocity? (c) Evaluate the result. Does it make sense?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-problems#fs-id1168329484447",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 3, Problem 54. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-problems#fs-id1168329484447\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
