    "category": "additional-problems",
    "topic": "Capacitance",
    "prompt": "Suppose that the capacitance of a variable capacitor can be manually changed from 100 to 800 pF by turning a dial connected to one set of plates by a shaft, from 0 ° to 180 ° . With the…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Suppose that the capacitance of a variable capacitor can be manually changed from 100 to 800 pF by turning a dial connected to one set of plates by a shaft, from <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>180</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. With the dial set at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>180</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> (corresponding to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>800</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>pF</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>), the capacitor is connected to a 500-V source. After charging, the capacitor is disconnected from the source, and the dial is turned to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. (a) What is the charge on the capacitor? (b) What is the voltage across the capacitor when the dial is set to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mtext>°</mtext><mtext>?</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Suppose that the capacitance of a variable capacitor can be manually changed from 100 to 800 pF by turning a dial connected to one set of plates by a shaft, from <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>180</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. With the dial set at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>180</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> (corresponding to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>800</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>pF</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>), the capacitor is connected to a 500-V source. After charging, the capacitor is disconnected from the source, and the dial is turned to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. (a) What is the charge on the capacitor? (b) What is the voltage across the capacitor when the dial is set to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mtext>°</mtext><mtext>?</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-additional-problems#fs-id1167131612839",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 8, Problem 66. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-additional-problems#fs-id1167131612839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
