    "number": 47,
    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Angular Momentum",
    "prompt": "A propeller consists of two blades each 3.0 m in length and mass 120 kg each. The propeller can be approximated by a single rod rotating about its center of mass. The propeller starts…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A propeller consists of two blades each 3.0 m in length and mass 120 kg each. The propeller can be approximated by a single rod rotating about its center of mass. The propeller starts from rest and rotates up to 1200 rpm in 30 seconds at a constant rate. (a) What is the angular momentum of the propeller at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s;</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s?</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> (b) What is the torque on the propeller?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A propeller consists of two blades each 3.0 m in length and mass 120 kg each. The propeller can be approximated by a single rod rotating about its center of mass. The propeller starts from rest and rotates up to 1200 rpm in 30 seconds at a constant rate. (a) What is the angular momentum of the propeller at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s;</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s?</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> (b) What is the torque on the propeller?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/11-problems#fs-id1165036993588",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-11#fs-id1165036993588-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>a. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>720.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>kg</mtext><mo>·</mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>; <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.20</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>rad</mtext></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><msup><mtext>s</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>;<span><br/></span>\n<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>42.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>rad</mtext></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>; <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.02</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>kg</mtext><mo>·</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>;<span><br/></span>\n<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>84.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>rad</mtext></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>;<span><br/></span>\nb. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.03</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mo>·</mo><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 11, Problem 47. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/11-problems#fs-id1165036993588\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
