    "prompt": "Examine the circuit shown below in part (a). Determine dI/dt at the instant after the switch is thrown in the circuit of (a), thereby producing the circuit of (b). Show that if I were to…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Examine the circuit shown below in part (a). Determine <em>dI/dt</em> at the instant after the switch is thrown in the circuit of (a), thereby producing the circuit of (b). Show that if <em>I</em> were to continue to increase at this initial rate, it would reach its maximum <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mtext>/</mtext><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> in one time constant.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Examine the circuit shown below in part (a). Determine <em>dI/dt</em> at the instant after the switch is thrown in the circuit of (a), thereby producing the circuit of (b). Show that if <em>I</em> were to continue to increase at this initial rate, it would reach its maximum <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mtext>/</mtext><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> in one time constant.</p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"Figure a shows a circuit with R and L in series with a battery, epsilon and a switch S1 which is open. Figure b shows a circuit with R and L in series with a battery, epsilon. The end of L that is connected to the positive terminal of the battery is at positive potential. Current flows through L from the positive end to the negative one.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/0868ec0e07304930b5e0a704.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-problems#fs-id1167131131840",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 14, Problem 56. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-problems#fs-id1167131131840\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
