    "number": 61,
    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Motion with Constant Acceleration",
    "prompt": "A woodpecker’s brain is specially protected from large accelerations by tendon-like attachments inside the skull. While pecking on a tree, the woodpecker’s head comes to a stop from an…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A woodpecker’s brain is specially protected from large accelerations by tendon-like attachments inside the skull. While pecking on a tree, the woodpecker’s head comes to a stop from an initial velocity of 0.600 m/s in a distance of only 2.00 mm. (a) Find the acceleration in meters per second squared <sup><!-- no-selfclose --></sup>and in multiples of <em>g</em>, where <em>g</em> = 9.80 m/s<sup>2</sup>. (b) Calculate the stopping time. (c) The tendons cradling the brain stretch, making its stopping distance 4.50 mm (greater than the head and, hence, less acceleration of the brain). What is the brain’s acceleration, expressed in multiples of <em>g</em>?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A woodpecker’s brain is specially protected from large accelerations by tendon-like attachments inside the skull. While pecking on a tree, the woodpecker’s head comes to a stop from an initial velocity of 0.600 m/s in a distance of only 2.00 mm. (a) Find the acceleration in meters per second squared <sup><!-- no-selfclose --></sup>and in multiples of <em>g</em>, where <em>g</em> = 9.80 m/s<sup>2</sup>. (b) Calculate the stopping time. (c) The tendons cradling the brain stretch, making its stopping distance 4.50 mm (greater than the head and, hence, less acceleration of the brain). What is the brain’s acceleration, expressed in multiples of <em>g</em>?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-problems#fs-id1168326972751",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-3#fs-id1168326972751-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>a. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9.18</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mi>g</mi><mo>;</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span><span><br/></span>\nb. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6.67</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−3</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>;<span><br/></span>\nc. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mtable><mtr></mtr><mtr></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>−</mtext><mn>40.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.08</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mi>g</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 3, Problem 61. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-problems#fs-id1168326972751\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
