    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Angular Momentum",
    "prompt": "A pulsar is a rapidly rotating neutron star. The Crab nebula pulsar in the constellation Taurus has a period of 33.5 × 10 −3 s , radius 10.0 km, and mass 2.8 × 10 30 kg . The pulsar’s…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A <span>pulsar</span> is a rapidly rotating neutron star. The Crab nebula pulsar in the constellation Taurus has a period of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>33.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−3</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, radius 10.0 km, and mass <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2.8</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>kg</mtext><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> The pulsar’s rotational period will increase over time due to the release of electromagnetic radiation, which doesn’t change its radius but reduces its rotational energy. (a) What is the angular momentum of the pulsar? (b) Suppose the angular velocity decreases at a rate of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−14</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>rad</mtext></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><msup><mtext>s</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. What is the torque on the pulsar?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A <span>pulsar</span> is a rapidly rotating neutron star. The Crab nebula pulsar in the constellation Taurus has a period of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>33.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−3</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, radius 10.0 km, and mass <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2.8</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>kg</mtext><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> The pulsar’s rotational period will increase over time due to the release of electromagnetic radiation, which doesn’t change its radius but reduces its rotational energy. (a) What is the angular momentum of the pulsar? (b) Suppose the angular velocity decreases at a rate of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−14</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>rad</mtext></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><msup><mtext>s</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. What is the torque on the pulsar?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/11-problems#fs-id1165037165011",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 11, Problem 48. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/11-problems#fs-id1165037165011\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
