    "source": "OpenStax",
    "volume": 2,
    "chapter": 7,
    "number": 79,
    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Applications of Electrostatics",
    "prompt": "(a) Will the electric field strength between two parallel conducting plates exceed the breakdown strength of dry air, which is 3.00 × 10 6 V/m , if the plates are separated by 2.00 mm…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>(a) Will the electric field strength between two parallel conducting plates exceed the breakdown strength of dry air, which is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3.00</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>6</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>V/m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, if the plates are separated by 2.00 mm and a potential difference of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is applied? (b) How close together can the plates be with this applied voltage?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>(a) Will the electric field strength between two parallel conducting plates exceed the breakdown strength of dry air, which is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3.00</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>6</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>V/m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, if the plates are separated by 2.00 mm and a potential difference of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is applied? (b) How close together can the plates be with this applied voltage?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-problems#fs-id1167586218763",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/chapter-7#fs-id1167586218763-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>a. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mtable><mtr></mtr><mtr></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>V/m</mtext><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>V/m</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>No, the field strength is smaller than the breakdown strength for air</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></semantics></math></span>;<span><br/></span>\nb. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.7</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>mm</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 7, Problem 79. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-problems#fs-id1167586218763\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
