    "prompt": "The traffic light hangs from the cables as shown. Draw a free-body diagram on a coordinate plane for this situation.",
    "previewHTML": "<p>The traffic light hangs from the cables as shown. Draw a free-body diagram on a coordinate plane for this situation.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>The traffic light hangs from the cables as shown. Draw a free-body diagram on a coordinate plane for this situation.</p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"Figure shows a traffic light hanging from a horizontal cable by three other cables, T1, T2 and T3. T1 hangs down and right making an angle of 41 degrees with the horizontal cable. T2 hangs down and left, making an angle of 63 degrees with the horizontal cable. These meet at a point and T3 hangs vertically down from here. The light is attached to T3. A vector pointing down from the light is labeled w equal to 200 newtons.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/c9547fb6a26c894dff8e034d.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-problems#fs-id1165039245852",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-5#fs-id1165039245852-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"Figure shows coordinate axes. Three arrows radiate out from the origin. T1, labeled 41 degrees points up and left. T2, labeled 63 degrees points up and right. T3 equal to w equal to 200 N is along the negative y axis.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/1ff0e5fb39e0619d8b9d9989.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 5, Problem 71. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-problems#fs-id1165039245852\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
