    "topic": "Current and resistance",
    "prompt": "Coils are often used in electrical and electronic circuits. Consider a coil which is formed by winding 1000 turns of insulated 20-gauge copper wire (area 0.52 mm 2 ) in a single layer on…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Coils are often used in electrical and electronic circuits. Consider a coil which is formed by winding 1000 turns of insulated 20-gauge copper wire (area <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>0.52</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>mm</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math></span> in a single layer on a cylindrical non-conducting core of radius 2.0 mm. What is the resistance of the coil? Neglect the thickness of the insulation.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Coils are often used in electrical and electronic circuits. Consider a coil which is formed by winding 1000 turns of insulated 20-gauge copper wire (area <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mn>0.52</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>mm</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math></span> in a single layer on a cylindrical non-conducting core of radius 2.0 mm. What is the resistance of the coil? Neglect the thickness of the insulation.</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-additional-problems#fs-id1170901594824",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 9, Problem 76. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-additional-problems#fs-id1170901594824\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
