    "number": 76,
    "category": "additional-problems",
    "topic": "Capacitance",
    "prompt": "A prankster applies 450 V to an 80.0 - μ F capacitor and then tosses it to an unsuspecting victim. The victim’s finger is burned by the discharge of the capacitor through 0.200 g of…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A prankster applies 450 V to an <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>80.0</mn><mtext>-</mtext><mi>μ</mi><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> capacitor and then tosses it to an unsuspecting victim. The victim’s finger is burned by the discharge of the capacitor through 0.200 g of flesh. Estimate, what is the temperature increase of the flesh? Is it reasonable to assume that no thermodynamic phase change happened?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A prankster applies 450 V to an <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>80.0</mn><mtext>-</mtext><mi>μ</mi><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> capacitor and then tosses it to an unsuspecting victim. The victim’s finger is burned by the discharge of the capacitor through 0.200 g of flesh. Estimate, what is the temperature increase of the flesh? Is it reasonable to assume that no thermodynamic phase change happened?</p>\n</div>",
