    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Solving Problems with Newton’s Laws",
    "prompt": "Three forces act on an object, considered to be a particle, which moves with constant velocity v = ( 3 i ^ − 2 j ^ ) m/s . Two of the forces are F → 1 = ( 3 i ^ + 5 j ^ ) N and F → 2 = (…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Three forces act on an object, considered to be a particle, which moves with constant velocity <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m/s</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> Two of the forces are <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> Find the third force.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Three forces act on an object, considered to be a particle, which moves with constant velocity <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m/s</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> Two of the forces are <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> Find the third force.</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-problems#fs-id1165037151556",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-6#fs-id1165037151556-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>\n<span><math display=\"inline\">\n<semantics>\n<mrow>\n<mrow>\n<msub>\n<mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\">\n<mover accent=\"true\">\n<mi>F</mi>\n<mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo>\n</mover>\n</mstyle>\n<mn>3</mn>\n</msub>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo>\n<mo>−</mo>\n<mn>7</mn>\n<mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\">\n<mover accent=\"true\">\n<mi>i</mi>\n<mo>^</mo>\n</mover>\n</mstyle>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mn>2</mn>\n<mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\">\n<mover accent=\"true\">\n<mi>j</mi>\n<mo>^</mo>\n</mover>\n</mstyle>\n<mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo>\n<mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace>\n<mtext>N</mtext>\n</mrow>\n</mrow>\n\n</semantics>\n</math></span>\n</p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 6, Problem 27. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-problems#fs-id1165037151556\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
