    "category": "additional-problems",
    "topic": "Work and energy",
    "prompt": "Consider a particle on which several forces act, one of which is known to be constant in time: F → 1 = ( 3 N ) i ^ + ( 4 N ) j ^ . As a result, the particle moves along a straight path…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Consider a particle on which several forces act, one of which is known to be constant in time: <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> As a result, the particle moves along a straight path from a Cartesian coordinate of (0 m, 0 m) to (5 m, 6 m). What is the work done by <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>\n?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Consider a particle on which several forces act, one of which is known to be constant in time: <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> As a result, the particle moves along a straight path from a Cartesian coordinate of (0 m, 0 m) to (5 m, 6 m). What is the work done by <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>\n?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-additional-problems#fs-id1165036765515",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-7#fs-id1165036765515-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>\n<span><math display=\"inline\">\n<semantics>\n<mrow>\n<mrow>\n<mn>39</mn>\n<mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace>\n<mtext>N</mtext>\n<mo>·</mo>\n<mtext>m</mtext>\n</mrow>\n</mrow>\n\n</semantics>\n</math></span>\n</p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 7, Problem 87. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-additional-problems#fs-id1165036765515\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
