    "number": 77,
    "category": "additional-problems",
    "topic": "Current and resistance",
    "prompt": "Currents of approximately 0.06 A can be potentially fatal. Currents in that range can make the heart fibrillate (beat in an uncontrolled manner). The resistance of a dry human body can…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Currents of approximately 0.06 A can be potentially fatal. Currents in that range can make the heart fibrillate (beat in an uncontrolled manner). The resistance of a dry human body can be approximately <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>k</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. (a) What voltage can cause 0.06 A through a dry human body? (b) When a human body is wet, the resistance can fall to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. What voltage can cause harm to a wet body?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Currents of approximately 0.06 A can be potentially fatal. Currents in that range can make the heart fibrillate (beat in an uncontrolled manner). The resistance of a dry human body can be approximately <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>k</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. (a) What voltage can cause 0.06 A through a dry human body? (b) When a human body is wet, the resistance can fall to <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. What voltage can cause harm to a wet body?</p></div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-additional-problems#fs-id1170899453567",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/chapter-9#fs-id1170899453567-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>a. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6000</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>; b. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p></div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 9, Problem 77. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-additional-problems#fs-id1170899453567\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
