    "category": "additional-problems",
    "topic": "Electric potential",
    "prompt": "Your friend gets really excited by the idea of making a lightning rod or maybe just a sparking toy by connecting two spheres as shown in Figure 7.39 , and making R 2 so small that the…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Your friend gets really excited by the idea of making a lightning rod or maybe just a sparking toy by connecting two spheres as shown in <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-5-equipotential-surfaces-and-conductors\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Figure 7.39</a>, and making <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> so small that the electric field is greater than the dielectric strength of air, just from the usual 150 V/m electric field near the surface of the Earth. If <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is 10 cm, how small does <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> need to be, and does this seem practical? (<em>Hint:</em> recall the calculation for electric field at the surface of a conductor from <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-introduction\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Gauss’s Law</a>.)</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Your friend gets really excited by the idea of making a lightning rod or maybe just a sparking toy by connecting two spheres as shown in <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-5-equipotential-surfaces-and-conductors\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Figure 7.39</a>, and making <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> so small that the electric field is greater than the dielectric strength of air, just from the usual 150 V/m electric field near the surface of the Earth. If <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is 10 cm, how small does <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> need to be, and does this seem practical? (<em>Hint:</em> recall the calculation for electric field at the surface of a conductor from <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-introduction\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Gauss’s Law</a>.)</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-additional-problems#fs-id1167586250282",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 7, Problem 96. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/7-additional-problems#fs-id1167586250282\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
