    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Potential Energy of a System",
    "prompt": "Using values from Table 8.1 , how many DNA molecules could be broken by the energy carried by a single electron in the beam of an old-fashioned TV tube? (These electrons were not…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Using values from <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-1-potential-energy-of-a-system\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Table 8.1</a>, how many DNA molecules could be broken by the energy carried by a single electron in the beam of an old-fashioned TV tube? (These electrons were not dangerous in themselves, but they did create dangerous X-rays. Later-model tube TVs had shielding that absorbed X-rays before they escaped and exposed viewers.)</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Using values from <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-1-potential-energy-of-a-system\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Table 8.1</a>, how many DNA molecules could be broken by the energy carried by a single electron in the beam of an old-fashioned TV tube? (These electrons were not dangerous in themselves, but they did create dangerous X-rays. Later-model tube TVs had shielding that absorbed X-rays before they escaped and exposed viewers.)</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-problems#fs-id1165035974202",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-8#fs-id1165035974202-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>40,000</p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 8, Problem 19. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-problems#fs-id1165035974202\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
