    "category": "additional-problems",
    "topic": "Electric charge and fields",
    "prompt": "Point charges are placed at the four corners of a rectangle as shown below: q 1 = 2.0 × 10 −6 C , q 2 = −2.0 × 10 −6 C, q 3 = 4.0 × 10 −6 C, and q 4 = 1.0 × 10 −6 C . What is the…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Point charges are placed at the four corners of a rectangle as shown below: <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−6</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>−2.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−6</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C,</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−6</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C,</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−6</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C</mtext><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> What is the electric field at <em>P</em>?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Point charges are placed at the four corners of a rectangle as shown below: <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−6</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>−2.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−6</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C,</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−6</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C,</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−6</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C</mtext><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> What is the electric field at <em>P</em>?</p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"A rectangle is shown with a charge at each corner. The rectangle is 4.0 centimeters high and 6.0 centimeters wide. At the top left is a positive charge q 1. At the top right is a negative charge q 2. At the lower left is a positive charge q 3. At the lower right is a positive charge q 4. Point P is in the middle of the upper edge, 3.0 centimeters to the right of q 1 and 3.0 centimeters to the left of q 2.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/622ba9a936a66c5813729594.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-additional-problems#fs-id1172098627206",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/chapter-5#fs-id1172098627206-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>\n<span><math display=\"inline\">\n<semantics>\n<mrow>\n<mrow>\n<msub>\n<mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\">\n<mover accent=\"true\">\n<mtext>E</mtext>\n<mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo>\n</mover>\n</mstyle>\n<mrow>\n<mtext>net</mtext>\n</mrow>\n</msub>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<msub>\n<mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\">\n<mover accent=\"true\">\n<mtext>E</mtext>\n<mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo>\n</mover>\n</mstyle>\n<mrow>\n<mn>1</mn>\n</mrow>\n</msub>\n</mrow>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<msub>\n<mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\">\n<mover accent=\"true\">\n<mtext>E</mtext>\n<mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo>\n</mover>\n</mstyle>\n<mrow>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</mrow>\n</msub>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<msub>\n<mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\">\n<mover accent=\"true\">\n<mtext>E</mtext>\n<mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo>\n</mover>\n</mstyle>\n<mrow>\n<mn>3</mn>\n</mrow>\n</msub>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<msub>\n<mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\">\n<mover accent=\"true\">\n<mtext>E</mtext>\n<mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo>\n</mover>\n</mstyle>\n<mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n</mrow>\n</msub>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mo>(</mo>\n<mn>4.65</mn>\n<mover accent=\"true\">\n<mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\">\n<mtext>i</mtext>\n</mstyle>\n<mo>^</mo>\n</mover>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mn>1.44</mn>\n<mover accent=\"true\">\n<mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\">\n<mtext>j</mtext>\n</mstyle>\n<mo>^</mo>\n</mover>\n<mo>)</mo>\n<mo>×</mo>\n<msup>\n<mrow>\n<mn>10</mn>\n</mrow>\n<mn>7</mn>\n</msup>\n<mo>N/C</mo>\n</mrow>\n\n</semantics>\n</math></span>\n</p></div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 5, Problem 117. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-additional-problems#fs-id1172098627206\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
