    "number": 65,
    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Conservation of Angular Momentum",
    "prompt": "Twin skaters approach one another as shown below and lock hands. (a) Calculate their final angular velocity, given each had an initial speed of 2.50 m/s relative to the ice. Each has a…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Twin skaters approach one another as shown below and lock hands. (a) Calculate their final angular velocity, given each had an initial speed of 2.50 m/s relative to the ice. Each has a mass of 70.0 kg, and each has a center of mass located 0.800 m from their locked hands. You may approximate their moments of inertia to be that of point masses at this radius. (b) Compare the initial kinetic energy and final kinetic energy.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Twin skaters approach one another as shown below and lock hands. (a) Calculate their final angular velocity, given each had an initial speed of 2.50 m/s relative to the ice. Each has a mass of 70.0 kg, and each has a center of mass located 0.800 m from their locked hands. You may approximate their moments of inertia to be that of point masses at this radius. (b) Compare the initial kinetic energy and final kinetic energy.</p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"Figure a, on the left, is a drawing of two ice skaters viewed from above moving with speed v toward each other along parallel lines. The upper one is skating to the right and the lower one to the left, and they are separated so that their hands will meet as they cross. Figure b, on the right, shows the skaters holding hands and moving together in a circle with angular velocity omega. Their motion is clockwise as viewed from above.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/41f94b9c6043f902ec8ebe48.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/11-problems#fs-id1165038040697",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-11#fs-id1165038040697-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>a. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>280</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>kg</mtext><mo>·</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>,<span><br/></span>\n<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>89.6</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>kg</mtext><mo>·</mo><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>,<span><br/></span>\n<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3.125</mn><mrow><mrow><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>rad</mtext></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>; b. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>437.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>J</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>,<span><br/></span>\n<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>f</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>437.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>J</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 11, Problem 65. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/11-problems#fs-id1165038040697\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
