    "prompt": "Is it possible to apply a force at P to keep in equilibrium the structure shown? The weight of the structure is negligible.",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Is it possible to apply a force at <em>P</em> to keep in equilibrium the structure shown? The weight of the structure is negligible.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Is it possible to apply a force at <em>P</em> to keep in equilibrium the structure shown? The weight of the structure is negligible.</p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"Figure shows the distribution of forces applied to point P. Force of 2000 N, two meters to the left of the point P, moves it downwards. Force of 3000 N, two meters to the right of the point P, moves it upwards. Force of 5000 N, two meters to the right and one meter above of the point P, moves it to the right.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/5e1d40cb4b542b747c82ae0a.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-problems#fs-id1163713483032",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 12, Problem 28. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-problems#fs-id1163713483032\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
