    "topic": "Gravitation and orbits",
    "prompt": "Following the technique used in Gravitation Near Earth’s Surface , find the value of g as a function of the radius r from the center of a spherical shell planet of constant density ρ…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Following the technique used in <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-2-gravitation-near-earths-surface\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Gravitation Near Earth’s Surface</a>, find the value of <em>g</em> as a function of the radius <em>r</em> from the center of a spherical shell planet of constant density <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></semantics></math></span> with inner and outer radii <sub><span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>\n</sub> and <sub><span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>out</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>\n</sub>. Find <em>g</em> for both <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>out</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and for <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. Assuming the inside of the shell is kept airless, describe travel inside the spherical shell planet.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Following the technique used in <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-2-gravitation-near-earths-surface\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Gravitation Near Earth’s Surface</a>, find the value of <em>g</em> as a function of the radius <em>r</em> from the center of a spherical shell planet of constant density <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></semantics></math></span> with inner and outer radii <sub><span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>\n</sub> and <sub><span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>out</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>\n</sub>. Find <em>g</em> for both <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>out</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and for <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. Assuming the inside of the shell is kept airless, describe travel inside the spherical shell planet.</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-challenge-problems#fs-id1168325911031",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 13, Problem 82. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-challenge-problems#fs-id1168325911031\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
