    "prompt": "A person with body resistance between their hands of 10.0 k Ω accidentally grasps the terminals of a 20.0-kV power supply. (Do NOT do this!) (a) Draw a circuit diagram to represent the…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A person with body resistance between their hands of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>10.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>k</mtext><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> accidentally grasps the terminals of a 20.0-kV power supply. (Do NOT do this!) (a) Draw a circuit diagram to represent the situation. (b) If the internal resistance of the power supply is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2000</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, what is the current through their body? (c) What is the power dissipated in their body? (d) If the power supply is to be made safe by increasing its internal resistance, what should the internal resistance be for the maximum current in this situation to be 1.00 mA or less? (e) Will this modification compromise the effectiveness of the power supply for driving low-resistance devices? Explain your reasoning.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A person with body resistance between their hands of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>10.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>k</mtext><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> accidentally grasps the terminals of a 20.0-kV power supply. (Do NOT do this!) (a) Draw a circuit diagram to represent the situation. (b) If the internal resistance of the power supply is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2000</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>Ω</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, what is the current through their body? (c) What is the power dissipated in their body? (d) If the power supply is to be made safe by increasing its internal resistance, what should the internal resistance be for the maximum current in this situation to be 1.00 mA or less? (e) Will this modification compromise the effectiveness of the power supply for driving low-resistance devices? Explain your reasoning.</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-problems#fs-id1167584991207",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 10, Problem 24. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-problems#fs-id1167584991207\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
