    "topic": "Rotation",
    "prompt": "The force of 20 j ^ N is applied at r → = ( 4.0 i ^ − 2.0 j ^ ) m . What is the torque of this force about the origin?",
    "previewHTML": "<p>The force of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is applied at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. What is the torque of this force about the origin?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>The force of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is applied at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. What is the torque of this force about the origin?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/10-additional-problems#fs-id1167131116074",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 10, Problem 118. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/10-additional-problems#fs-id1167131116074\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
