    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Electric Flux",
    "prompt": "Consider the uniform electric field E → = ( 4.0 j ^ + 3.0 k ^ ) × 10 3 N/C . What is its electric flux through a circular area of radius 2.0 m that lies in the xy -plane?",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Consider the uniform electric field <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>E</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>j</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>k</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N/C</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> What is its electric flux through a circular area of radius 2.0 m that lies in the <em>xy</em>-plane?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Consider the uniform electric field <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>E</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>j</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>k</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N/C</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> What is its electric flux through a circular area of radius 2.0 m that lies in the <em>xy</em>-plane?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-problems#fs-id1172100624187",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/chapter-6#fs-id1172100624187-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>\n<span><math display=\"inline\">\n<semantics>\n<mrow>\n<mrow>\n<mtext>Φ</mtext>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mn>3.8</mn>\n<mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace>\n<mo>×</mo>\n<mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace>\n<msup>\n<mrow>\n<mn>10</mn>\n</mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n</msup>\n<mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace>\n<mtext>N</mtext>\n<mo>·</mo>\n<msup>\n<mtext>m</mtext>\n<mn>2</mn>\n</msup>\n<mtext>/</mtext>\n<mtext>C</mtext>\n</mrow>\n</mrow>\n\n</semantics>\n</math></span>\n</p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 6, Problem 27. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-problems#fs-id1172100624187\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
