    "category": "additional-problems",
    "topic": "Newton’s laws",
    "prompt": "Shown below is a body of mass 1.0 kg under the influence of the forces F → A , F → B , and m g → . If the body accelerates to the left at 20 m/s 2 , what are F → A and F → B ?",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Shown below is a body of mass 1.0 kg under the influence of the forces <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>A</mi></mstyle></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>B</mi></mstyle></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. If the body accelerates to the left at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, what are <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>A</mi></mstyle></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>B</mi></mstyle></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Shown below is a body of mass 1.0 kg under the influence of the forces <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>A</mi></mstyle></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>B</mi></mstyle></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. If the body accelerates to the left at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, what are <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>A</mi></mstyle></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>B</mi></mstyle></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>?</p><span>\n<img alt=\"Three arrows radiate outwards from a point labeled m. F subscript A points left and down, making an angle of 60 degrees with the negative x axis. F subscript B points left and up, making an angle of minus 30 degrees with the negative x axis. Vector mg points vertically down.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"textbook-assets/78ed149f6b17b487d5ce2c39.jpg\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-additional-problems#fs-id1165039453995",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 5, Problem 82. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-additional-problems#fs-id1165039453995\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
