    "number": 72,
    "category": "additional-problems",
    "topic": "Static equilibrium",
    "prompt": "A 40-kg boy jumps from a height of 3.0 m, lands on one foot and comes to rest in 0.10 s after he hits the ground. Assume that he comes to rest with a constant acceleration opposite to…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A 40-kg boy jumps from a height of 3.0 m, lands on one foot and comes to rest in 0.10 s after he hits the ground. Assume that he comes to rest with a constant acceleration opposite to the motion. If the total cross-sectional area of the bones in his legs just above his ankles is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> what is the compression stress in these bones? Leg bones can be fractured when they are subjected to stress greater than <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1.7</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>8</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>Pa</mtext><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> Is the boy in danger of breaking his leg?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A 40-kg boy jumps from a height of 3.0 m, lands on one foot and comes to rest in 0.10 s after he hits the ground. Assume that he comes to rest with a constant acceleration opposite to the motion. If the total cross-sectional area of the bones in his legs just above his ankles is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> what is the compression stress in these bones? Leg bones can be fractured when they are subjected to stress greater than <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1.7</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mn>8</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>Pa</mtext><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> Is the boy in danger of breaking his leg?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-additional-problems#fs-id1163713006027",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 12, Problem 72. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-additional-problems#fs-id1163713006027\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
