    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Sources of Energy",
    "prompt": "Shown below is a box of mass m 1 that sits on a frictionless incline at an angle above the horizontal θ = 30 ° . This box is connected by a relatively massless string, over a…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Shown below is a box of mass <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> that sits on a frictionless incline at an angle above the horizontal <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo></mrow></semantics></math></span>. This box is connected by a relatively massless string, over a frictionless pulley, and finally connected to a box at rest over the ledge, labeled <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. If <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> are a height <em>h</em> above the ground and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;&gt;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>: (a) What is the initial gravitational potential energy of the system? (b) What is the final kinetic energy of the system when <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math></span> hits the ground?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Shown below is a box of mass <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> that sits on a frictionless incline at an angle above the horizontal <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo></mrow></semantics></math></span>. This box is connected by a relatively massless string, over a frictionless pulley, and finally connected to a box at rest over the ledge, labeled <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>. If <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> are a height <em>h</em> above the ground and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;&gt;</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>: (a) What is the initial gravitational potential energy of the system? (b) What is the final kinetic energy of the system when <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math></span> hits the ground?</p><span>\n<img alt=\"A block, labeled as m sub1, is on an upward sloping ramp that makes an angle theta to the horizontal. The mass is connected to a string that goes up and over a pulley at the top of the ramp, then straight down and connects to another block, labeled as m sub 2. Block m sub 2 is not in contact with any surface.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/640bdf7a8fabb274234a193c42cca1cf9f9bcb77\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-problems#fs-id1165037857365",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 8, Problem 68. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-problems#fs-id1165037857365\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
