    "topic": "Potential energy",
    "prompt": "A particle of mass 2.0 kg moves under the influence of the force F ( x ) = ( 5 x 2 − 7 x ) N . Suppose a frictional force also acts on the particle. If the particle’s speed when it…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A particle of mass 2.0 kg moves under the influence of the force <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> Suppose a frictional force also acts on the particle. If the particle’s speed when it starts at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>−4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is 0.0 m/s and when it arrives at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is 9.0 m/s, how much work is done on it by the frictional force between <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>−4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m?</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A particle of mass 2.0 kg moves under the influence of the force <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> Suppose a frictional force also acts on the particle. If the particle’s speed when it starts at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>−4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is 0.0 m/s and when it arrives at <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> is 9.0 m/s, how much work is done on it by the frictional force between <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>−4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m?</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-additional-problems#fs-id1165038011534",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 8, Problem 70. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-additional-problems#fs-id1165038011534\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
