    "topic": "Acceleration Vector",
    "prompt": "The acceleration of a particle is a constant. At t = 0 the velocity of the particle is ( 10 i ^ + 20 j ^ ) m/s . At t = 4 s the velocity is 10 j ^ m/s . (a) What is the particle’s…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>The acceleration of a particle is a constant. At <em>t</em> = 0 the velocity of the particle is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>20</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> At <em>t</em> = 4 s the velocity is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> (a) What is the particle’s acceleration? (b) How do the position and velocity vary with time? Assume the particle is initially at the origin.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>The acceleration of a particle is a constant. At <em>t</em> = 0 the velocity of the particle is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mn>20</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> At <em>t</em> = 4 s the velocity is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mrow><mtext>m/s</mtext></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> (a) What is the particle’s acceleration? (b) How do the position and velocity vary with time? Assume the particle is initially at the origin.</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-problems#fs-id1165038334612",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 4, Problem 30. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-problems#fs-id1165038334612\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
