    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Acceleration Vector",
    "prompt": "A particle has a position function r → ( t ) = cos ( 1.0 t ) i ^ + sin ( 1.0 t ) j ^ + t k ^ , where the arguments of the cosine and sine functions are in radians. (a) What is the…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A particle has a position function <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext>cos</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mtext>sin</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> where the arguments of the cosine and sine functions are in radians. (a) What is the velocity vector? (b) What is the acceleration vector?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A particle has a position function <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext>cos</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mtext>sin</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> where the arguments of the cosine and sine functions are in radians. (a) What is the velocity vector? (b) What is the acceleration vector?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-problems#fs-id1165038303652",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-4#fs-id1165038303652-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>a. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext>−sin</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mtext>cos</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>, b. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext>−cos</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mtext>sin</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 4, Problem 31. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-problems#fs-id1165038303652\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
