    "number": 74,
    "category": "additional-problems",
    "topic": "Potential energy",
    "prompt": "An object of mass 10 kg is released at point A , slides to the bottom of the 30 ° incline, then collides with a horizontal massless spring, compressing it a maximum distance of 0.75 m.…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>An object of mass 10 kg is released at point <em>A</em>, slides to the bottom of the <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> incline, then collides with a horizontal massless spring, compressing it a maximum distance of 0.75 m. (See below.) The spring constant is 500 N/m, the height of the incline is 2.0 m, and the horizontal surface is frictionless. (a) What is the speed of the object at the bottom of the incline? (b) What is the work of friction on the object while it is on the incline? (c) The spring recoils and sends the object back toward the incline. What is the speed of the object when it reaches the base of the incline? (d) What vertical distance does it move back up the incline?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>An object of mass 10 kg is released at point <em>A</em>, slides to the bottom of the <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> incline, then collides with a horizontal massless spring, compressing it a maximum distance of 0.75 m. (See below.) The spring constant is 500 N/m, the height of the incline is 2.0 m, and the horizontal surface is frictionless. (a) What is the speed of the object at the bottom of the incline? (b) What is the work of friction on the object while it is on the incline? (c) The spring recoils and sends the object back toward the incline. What is the speed of the object when it reaches the base of the incline? (d) What vertical distance does it move back up the incline?</p>\n<span>\n<img alt=\"A block is shown at the top of a downward sloping ramp. The ramp makes an angle of 30 degrees with the horizontal. The block is a vertical distance of 2.0 meters above the ground. To the right of the ramp, on the horizontal ground, is a  horizontal spring. The far end of the spring is attached to a wall.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/resources/d6d62012dc13122025eebd0dd1eb7004fcdb484d\"/>\n</span>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-additional-problems#fs-id1165037262240",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 8, Problem 74. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-additional-problems#fs-id1165037262240\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
