    "topic": "Conductors in Electrostatic Equilibrium",
    "prompt": "Two parallel plates 10 cm on a side are given equal and opposite charges of magnitude 5.0 × 10 −9 C . The plates are 1.5 mm apart. What is the electric field at the center of the region…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Two parallel plates 10 cm on a side are given equal and opposite charges of magnitude <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−9</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> The plates are 1.5 mm apart. What is the electric field at the center of the region between the plates?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Two parallel plates 10 cm on a side are given equal and opposite charges of magnitude <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>−9</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>C</mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> The plates are 1.5 mm apart. What is the electric field at the center of the region between the plates?</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-problems#fs-id1172100905093",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/chapter-6#fs-id1172100905093-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>\n<span><math display=\"inline\">\n<semantics>\n<mrow>\n<mrow>\n<mi>E</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mn>5.65</mn>\n<mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace>\n<mo>×</mo>\n<mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace>\n<msup>\n<mrow>\n<mn>10</mn>\n</mrow>\n<mn>4</mn>\n</msup>\n<mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace>\n<mrow>\n<mtext>N</mtext>\n<mtext>/</mtext>\n<mtext>C</mtext>\n</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</mrow>\n\n</semantics>\n</math></span>\n</p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 6, Problem 67. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-problems#fs-id1172100905093\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
