    "number": 49,
    "category": "problems",
    "topic": "Projectile Motion",
    "prompt": "An astronaut on Mars kicks a soccer ball at an angle of 45 ° with an initial velocity of 15 m/s. If the acceleration of gravity on Mars is 3.7 m/ s 2 , (a) what is the range of the…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>An astronaut on Mars kicks a soccer ball at an angle of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>45</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> with an initial velocity of 15 m/s. If the acceleration of gravity on Mars is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.7</mn><mtext>m/</mtext><msup><mtext>s</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math></span>, (a) what is the range of the soccer kick on a flat surface? (b) What would be the range of the same kick on the Moon, where gravity is one-sixth that of Earth?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>An astronaut on Mars kicks a soccer ball at an angle of <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>45</mn><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> with an initial velocity of 15 m/s. If the acceleration of gravity on Mars is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.7</mn><mtext>m/</mtext><msup><mtext>s</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math></span>, (a) what is the range of the soccer kick on a flat surface? (b) What would be the range of the same kick on the Moon, where gravity is one-sixth that of Earth?</p></div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-problems#fs-id1165166487901",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-4#fs-id1165166487901-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>a. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60.8</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span>,<span><br/></span>\nb. <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>137.8</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 4, Problem 49. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-problems#fs-id1165166487901\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
