    "category": "additional-problems",
    "topic": "Sources of magnetic fields",
    "prompt": "A wire element has d l → , I d l → = J A d l = J d v , where A and dv are the cross-sectional area and volume of the element, respectively. Use this, the Biot-Savart law, and J = n e v…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A wire element has <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>J</mi></mstyle><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>J</mi></mstyle><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> where <em>A</em> and <em>dv</em> are the cross-sectional area and volume of the element, respectively. Use this, the Biot-Savart law, and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>J</mi></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>v</mi></mstyle></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> to show that the magnetic field of a moving point charge q is given by:<span><br/></span>\n<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>v</mi></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A wire element has <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>J</mi></mstyle><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>J</mi></mstyle><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> where <em>A</em> and <em>dv</em> are the cross-sectional area and volume of the element, respectively. Use this, the Biot-Savart law, and <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>J</mi></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>v</mi></mstyle></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> to show that the magnetic field of a moving point charge q is given by:<span><br/></span>\n<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mi>v</mi></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mo>×</mo><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/12-additional-problems#fs-id1166168318816",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 12, Problem 74. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/12-additional-problems#fs-id1166168318816\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
