    "prompt": "Show that the magnetic field of a thin wire and that of a current loop are zero if you are infinitely far away.",
    "previewHTML": "<p>Show that the magnetic field of a thin wire and that of a current loop are zero if you are infinitely far away.</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>Show that the magnetic field of a thin wire and that of a current loop are zero if you are infinitely far away.</p>\n</div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/12-additional-problems#fs-id1166164827210",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/chapter-12#fs-id1166164827210-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p>As the radial distance goes to infinity, the magnetic fields of each of these formulae go to zero.</p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 2</em>, Chapter 12, Problem 83. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/12-additional-problems#fs-id1166164827210\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
