    "topic": "Motion in two dimensions",
    "prompt": "A particle’s centripetal acceleration is a C = 4.0 m / s 2 at t = 0 s where it is on the x -axis and moving counterclockwise in the xy plane. It is executing uniform circular motion…",
    "previewHTML": "<p>A particle’s centripetal acceleration is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>C</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mtext>m</mtext><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><msup><mtext>s</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> at <em>t</em> = 0 s where it is on the <em>x</em>-axis and moving counterclockwise in the <em>xy</em> plane. It is executing uniform circular motion about an axis at a distance of 5.0 m. What is its velocity at <em>t</em> = 10 s?</p>",
    "statementHTML": "<div>\n<p>A particle’s centripetal acceleration is <span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>C</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mtext>m</mtext><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><msup><mtext>s</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span> at <em>t</em> = 0 s where it is on the <em>x</em>-axis and moving counterclockwise in the <em>xy</em> plane. It is executing uniform circular motion about an axis at a distance of 5.0 m. What is its velocity at <em>t</em> = 10 s?</p></div>",
    "url": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-additional-problems#fs-id1165039309472",
    "solutionUrl": "https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/chapter-4#fs-id1165039309472-solution",
    "solutionHTML": "<div>\n<p><span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext>−</mtext><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>cos</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>sin</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.5em\"></mspace></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span><span><br/></span>\n<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5.0</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mtext>m</mtext><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.5em\"></mspace><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.89</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>rad</mtext></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span><span><br/></span>\n<span><math display=\"inline\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo></mover></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>−2.24</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mtext>m</mtext><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><mtext>s</mtext><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3.87</mn><mspace width=\"0.2em\"></mspace><mrow><mtext>m</mtext><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><mtext>s</mtext><mstyle mathvariant=\"bold\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow></semantics></math></span></p>\n</div>",
    "attributionHTML": "OpenStax, <em>University Physics Volume 1</em>, Chapter 4, Problem 87. Authors: William Moebs, Samuel J. Ling, and Jeff Sanny. <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-additional-problems#fs-id1165039309472\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Access for free at OpenStax</a>. Content © Rice University, licensed <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. Formatting adapted for this noncommercial local study app."
